    "result_text": "All 272 tests pass. The change is complete.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe requirement was to store telephone numbers as digits only, return the stored (normalized) value, and normalize before the uniqueness check so `(613) 555-0100` and `6135550100` collide.\n\nTwo changes:\n\n1. **`src/main/resources/openapi.yml`** \u2014 Relaxed the `telephone` validation pattern from `^[0-9]*$` to `^[0-9 ()-]*$`. The DTO validation (`@Valid @RequestBody`) runs at the controller boundary before any of my code, so the formatted input had to be accepted there or it would be rejected with a 400 before normalization could happen.\n\n2. **`src/main/java/.../model/Owner.java`** \u2014 `setTelephone()` now strips spaces, dashes and parentheses (`replaceAll(\"[\\\\s()-]\", \"\")`). Placing normalization in the entity setter means:\n   - The stored value is digits-only, so `GET` returns it in the `telephone` field as digits (satisfies `coreStoresDigitsOnly`).\n   - Both the create path (MapStruct mapper calls the setter) and the update path (`currentOwner.setTelephone(...)`) are covered.\n   - The service-layer `isDuplicateTelephone` check reads `getTelephone()`, which is already normalized \u2014 so `(613) 555-0100` is detected as a duplicate of `6135550100` (satisfies `functionalityNormalisedDuplicateRejected`).\n   - The entity's existing `@Pattern(\"^[0-9]{10}$\")` still passes since a valid 10-digit number remains 10 digits after stripping.\n\nThe new `Cp11Tests` (both cases) pass, along with the full suite (272 tests, 0 failures).",
